WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.800
The Matabele ant’s strict termite diet

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.480
means many of these ants’ days are spent

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.920
in dangerous raids on these combative insects.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:11.000
The termites are no pushovers,

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.860
which leaves many ants wounded after each raid.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.140
Researchers recently found that injured ants are often

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.820
carried back to the nest by their fellow soldiers.

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:24.500
Now they have discovered that once back at the nest,

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:27.340
the injured ants' wounds are treated by their nestmates,

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:29.420
who spend time grooming the open wounds

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.000
to clear debris, adding antimicrobial chemicals,

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.520
and removing any pesky termites.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.780
And the treatment works.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:39.760
Ants treated within the first hour

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.080
had a mortality rate of 10%,

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.000
while ants that received no treatment

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.840
had a mortality rate of 80%.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.540
Researchers found that lightly injured ants altered their

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:53.239
behavior when near a returning column of ants.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.880
When nest-mates were close, they slowed their pace

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.940
dramatically, signaling that they’re injured

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:01.240
If the ants passed them by without help,

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.360
the injured ant immediately started walking faster,

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:07.060
following them back to the nest.

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.220
Ants that lost five or more limbs in a termite battle

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:13.000
were rarely helped back to the nest.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.640
Normally, when an injured ant is found,

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:20.620
it curls up into a ball to allow for easy transport.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.360
Heavily injured ants, however,

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:24.360
flail their remaining limbs about

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:25.820
and spin on their backs,

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:30.220
becoming so uncooperative that they’re left behind.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.640
Moving forward, researchers hope to find out

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.540
if this grooming behavior simply prevents infection

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:39.120
after termite skirmishes or if it could also be

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.360
an effective treatment if an infectious disease

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.920
attacks  the colony.

